<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
        Hardened-runtime entitlements for Coincube. Applied at codesign
        time by `rcodesign sign`, pointed here with its entitlements XML
        path flag (or by `codesign` with its entitlements flag). See
        contrib/release/macos/README.md for the full codesign and
        notarize workflow.

        NEVER write a double hyphen inside these comments, not even inside
        a flag name. XML forbids it, and while `plutil` and rcodesign's
        parser both accept it, Apple's AMFI parser does not: `codesign`
        rejects the whole file with "AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error"
        and signs nothing. A flag name written out in full cost us
        exactly that, and the failure names a line number in a comment,
        which reads like a corrupt file rather than a prose typo.

        Network entitlements are required for the local LAN signer
        (`phone_signer` module): we open a TCP listener during pairing,
        we dial the phone's TLS endpoint at steady state, and we
        multicast for the mDNS browse/advertise.
    -->

    <!-- Accept inbound TCP connections (pairing listener). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Dial outbound TCP connections (PhoneSigner steady-state). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
        Data-protection keychain access group for the device secret.

        The value is `<TeamID>.<bundleID>` and must stay in sync with
        CFBundleIdentifier in the Info.plist baked into `_coincube.zip`
        (io.coincube.tenshu). Team ID 8UVR249AD5 is written literally on
        purpose: `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)` and `$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)` are
        Xcode build-setting substitutions, and `rcodesign` signs this file
        verbatim without expanding anything — a signature carrying the raw
        variable string fails at runtime looking exactly like a keychain bug.

        The Rust side does not set kSecAttrAccessGroup, so items land in the
        first group listed here. An access group is part of a keychain item's
        identity: once a signed, entitled build has shipped, changing this
        value strands every device secret written under the old group.
    -->
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>8UVR249AD5.io.coincube.tenshu</string>
    </array>

    <!--
        WebAuthn relying party for macOS passkey unlock. Also restricted, and
        also authorised by the embedded provisioning profile (which grants
        `com.apple.developer.associated-domains` as a wildcard).

        The domain must match the AASA served from coincube.io, whose
        `webcredentials.apps` array already lists
        8UVR249AD5.io.coincube.tenshu, and the RP ID the app passes to
        AuthenticationServices (`COINCUBE_PASSKEY_RP_ID`, which still defaults
        to "localhost" — see services/passkey/mod.rs).

        Inert until COINCUBE_ENABLE_PASSKEY is on: nothing requests a passkey
        ceremony today, so this only makes the capability available to a build
        that does.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
    <array>
        <string>webcredentials:coincube.io</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
